 The packer records the rewritten module files and their tables in `DMModuleFiles`, and rejects a module without its fixups table. Before starting the host, the launcher checks that every recorded file is readable and is a regular file, so losing a binary and its table together still produces the missing-data alert. The alert passes the game name and message as AppleScript arguments, preserving quotes and backslashes in game names.
 
-The launcher sets the host's environment from the bundle, as the iOS app's `main.m` does, and runs the host as a child process. The save goes to the game folder, the folder holding the `.app`, as `Saves/save` (`DM_SAVE`), and screenshots and recordings to its `Screenshots` and `Recordings` folders (`DM_GAME_FOLDER`), so everything of a game sits beside it. The first time, a save in `~/Library/Application Support/nxnative/<title ID>/save` is copied there (into a temporary folder beside it, then renamed into place); the old one is never moved or deleted, and when both hold a save the game folder's is used and neither is touched. Where the game folder is no place for them (not writable, an Applications folder, or the read-only copy macOS runs a quarantined app from), or with `DM_GAME_FOLDER=0` in an `env.txt`, the save stays in Application Support and captures go to `~/Pictures/NXForge` and `~/Movies/NXForge`. `pso_user.tsv` (`DM_PSO_USER`, with `pso_archive/` beside it) and temporary files go to `~/Library/Application Support/nxnative/<title ID>/` (`temp/`; that folder is also `DM_ROOT` and the working folder). The host's output goes to `~/Library/Logs/nxnative/<title ID>.log`, and the previous run's is kept as `<title ID>.prev.log`. Environment switches go in `env.txt` in the Application Support folder, then in the bundle's `dm/env.txt` (one `KEY=VALUE` per line). Neither replaces a variable that is already set. Then `dm/mac/Launcher.swift` (sharing the iOS launcher's `LauncherModel` and `Resources/dm/launcher.json`) resolves the player's saved choices (`launcher.<VAR>` in the app's preferences domain, checked against `launcher.json`; `DM_SAVED_CHOICES=0` ignores them) and `launcher.json`'s tested defaults ([Saved choices](frontends.md#saved-choices)). Unless `DM_LAUNCHER=0`, `--no-launcher` or **Show this window at launch** turned off skips it, the launcher shows the settings window in its own process ([The Mac settings window](settings.md#the-mac-settings-window)); when skipped, the same resolved choices and defaults go into the environment without writing preferences. The launcher's C and Swift sources and the Swift compiler are in the app's reuse key. Cheat files go in `cheats/` in the Application Support folder ([cheats](cheats.md)). The launcher passes SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP on to the host and returns the host's exit code. When the bundle's game data is missing, it shows an alert.
+The launcher sets the host's environment from the bundle, as the iOS app's `main.m` does, and runs the host as a child process. The save goes to the game folder, the folder holding the `.app`, as `Saves/save` (`DM_SAVE`), and screenshots and recordings to its `Screenshots` and `Recordings` folders (`DM_GAME_FOLDER`), so everything of a game sits beside it. The first time, a save in `~/Library/Application Support/nxnative/<title ID>/save` is copied there (into a temporary folder beside it, then renamed into place); the old one is never moved or deleted, and when both hold a save the game folder's is used and neither is touched. Where the game folder is no place for them (not writable, an Applications folder, or the read-only copy macOS runs a quarantined app from), or with `DM_GAME_FOLDER=0` in an `env.txt`, the save stays in Application Support and captures go to `~/Pictures/NXForge` and `~/Movies/NXForge`. `pso_user.tsv` (`DM_PSO_USER`, with `pso_archive/` beside it) and temporary files go to `~/Library/Application Support/nxnative/<title ID>/` (`temp/`; that folder is also `DM_ROOT` and the working folder). The host's output goes to `~/Library/Logs/nxnative/<title ID>.log`, and the previous run's is kept as `<title ID>.prev.log`. Environment switches go in `env.txt` in the Application Support folder, then in the bundle's `dm/env.txt` (one `KEY=VALUE` per line). Neither replaces a variable that is already set. Then `dm/mac/Launcher.swift` (sharing the iOS launcher's `LauncherModel` and `Resources/dm/launcher.json`) resolves the player's saved choices (`launcher.<VAR>` in the app's preferences domain, checked against `launcher.json`; `DM_SAVED_CHOICES=0` ignores them) and `launcher.json`'s tested defaults ([Saved choices](frontends.md#saved-choices)). Before that, unless `DM_LAUNCHER=0` says otherwise, the launcher runs the cloud save check in its own process (`dm/mac/nxforge/launcher_save_check.swift`, upstream's `SaveCheck`); NXForge's apps show no settings window, since NXForge.app writes the saved choices ([The Mac settings window](settings.md#the-mac-settings-window)), so the resolved choices and defaults go into the environment without writing preferences. The launcher's C and Swift sources and the Swift compiler are in the app's reuse key. Cheat files go in `cheats/` in the Application Support folder ([cheats](cheats.md)). The launcher passes SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP on to the host and returns the host's exit code. When the bundle's game data is missing, it shows an alert.
 
 Why the host is a child process: macOS privacy checks (TCC) cover files on removable volumes. A launcher that hands its process over to the host, such as a shell script that `exec`s `dmhost`, got EPERM on every open on the external drive, with no prompt. That happened with ad-hoc and Developer ID signatures alike, and after `tccutil reset`. A compiled launcher as `CFBundleExecutable` that `posix_spawn`s `dmhost`, which sits beside it in `Contents/MacOS`, can read those files. An app that sits on the game drive needs this, and so does an env file that points at files there.
 
